WEBVTT

00:00.691 --> 00:17.102
[SPEAKER_01]: We did a little meditation on relative bodhicitta and now to focus and meditate a little bit on absolute bodhicitta.

00:19.544 --> 00:28.110
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolute bodhicitta is meditating on I think we can say two natures of

00:31.555 --> 00:42.843
[SPEAKER_01]: all the phenomena outer phenomena understanding resting and understanding of true nature of outer and inner outer phenomena and inner

00:51.796 --> 00:58.200
[SPEAKER_01]: So there are also many different methods or different teachings about that.

00:59.461 --> 01:17.434
[SPEAKER_01]: So one way to do is to see everything, all the phenomena that appears to oneself as nothing but a dream or illusion.

01:22.605 --> 01:51.173
[SPEAKER_01]: all outer phenomena as well as all the sentient beings everything nothing but a dream nothing exists outside but everything projection of one's own mind so people who are quite familiar with Mahayana Sutras will understand

01:55.344 --> 01:58.886
[SPEAKER_01]: how to meditate on emptiness.

02:06.111 --> 02:16.017
[SPEAKER_01]: Also in Buddha's teaching like Heart Sutra and all that explains quite detailedly how to meditate.

02:18.533 --> 02:32.846
[SPEAKER_01]: focusing detailedly, emphasize on emptiness, which actually I think talking about all phenomena is not real, but it is on mind projection, it's all like a dream, dream and illusion.

02:35.368 --> 02:43.676
[SPEAKER_01]: So later, like great scholars like Nagarjuna explained more detailed

02:45.718 --> 02:50.003
[SPEAKER_01]: way to how to analyze all the outer phenomena.

02:52.786 --> 02:59.073
[SPEAKER_01]: Through analytic then we will find everything nothing but emptiness.

03:00.775 --> 03:03.418
[SPEAKER_01]: So there are many different ways to do that also.

03:11.676 --> 03:18.120
[SPEAKER_01]: the most elaborate ways to do that is the teaching of interdependent.

03:20.902 --> 03:25.165
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is nothing but interdependent and everything is nothing but emptiness.

03:30.288 --> 03:31.269
[SPEAKER_01]: So if we sort of...

03:36.732 --> 03:42.035
[SPEAKER_01]: look in quite a simple ways to look at things.

03:42.715 --> 03:43.436
[SPEAKER_01]: Next example.

03:46.638 --> 03:56.903
[SPEAKER_01]: If we see, take an example of incense, a long incense and then the short incense.

03:58.604 --> 04:03.647
[SPEAKER_01]: So when you see the long incense you will say this is long and this is a short incense.

04:05.679 --> 04:15.830
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you bring even longer incense, incense stick, then that long incense becomes a short incense.

04:17.712 --> 04:23.498
[SPEAKER_01]: So you call it one is long and one is short because in relation.

04:30.067 --> 04:32.649
[SPEAKER_01]: in relation, not in itself.

04:33.409 --> 04:45.738
[SPEAKER_01]: So same thing, I think many things we label in outer world, good, bad, beautiful, not beautiful, pleasant, unpleasant.

04:47.239 --> 04:50.181
[SPEAKER_01]: It is mostly we label them.

04:51.261 --> 04:54.844
[SPEAKER_01]: It does not exist independently.

04:57.285 --> 04:57.826
[SPEAKER_01]: On is on.

05:01.869 --> 05:14.520
[SPEAKER_01]: So therefore, when you analyze like that, all very detailly, most of the things that we perceive, it does not exist by its own.

05:15.641 --> 05:23.809
[SPEAKER_01]: So therefore, when you analyze through analyzing, some kind of understanding,

05:25.646 --> 05:29.569
[SPEAKER_01]: all the phenomena and nothing but its own kind of mind projection.

05:31.190 --> 05:46.080
[SPEAKER_01]: So trying to rest in this kind of state of mind and then also we can look back into our mind.

05:50.382 --> 05:53.985
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it really there is something called mind?

05:57.467 --> 06:07.431
[SPEAKER_01]: then the past thoughts, future thoughts, present thoughts, and if past and present meet, it becomes present.

06:08.212 --> 06:12.294
[SPEAKER_01]: If the future thoughts and present thoughts meet, it becomes future.

06:13.214 --> 06:23.939
[SPEAKER_01]: So therefore if you analyze stream of mind, you will also not find such thing as solid entity as a mind.

06:25.319 --> 06:26.300
[SPEAKER_01]: So when we meditate,

06:28.236 --> 06:29.557
[SPEAKER_01]: There's two kinds of meditation.

06:30.937 --> 06:38.601
[SPEAKER_01]: One is what we call analytic, analyzing all the outer phenomena, analyzing all the inner state of mind.

06:40.322 --> 06:51.367
[SPEAKER_01]: Through investigating and analyzing, you will reach to a certain kind of understanding or certain kind of small experience.

06:53.248 --> 06:56.810
[SPEAKER_01]: And then trying to rest without fabricating

06:58.857 --> 07:01.739
[SPEAKER_01]: just sort of remain in that state.

07:04.440 --> 07:17.668
[SPEAKER_01]: It's called what we call ultimate bodhicitta because how this is the how all outer and inner absolute truth is.

07:19.229 --> 07:26.573
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's kind of brief introduction on how to sort of rest

07:28.158 --> 07:31.740
[SPEAKER_01]: state in meditation of Absolute Bodhicitta.

07:34.222 --> 07:54.695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in order to actually put in practice what Rinpoche explained on the Absolute Bodhicitta, as Rinpoche mentioned, first we go to an analytical process, taking first out of phenomena,

07:56.782 --> 08:03.508
[SPEAKER_00]: She gave the example of the notion of short and long, which doesn't exist in themselves, they're just relative.

08:05.089 --> 08:09.774
[SPEAKER_00]: But even anything that we take on its own, like a table.

08:11.175 --> 08:15.879
[SPEAKER_00]: If we break it into pieces, just pieces of wood, it's no more what we call a table.

08:16.800 --> 08:22.525
[SPEAKER_00]: And even a piece of wood, we may put it into sawdust, it's just wood dust.

08:23.359 --> 08:50.212
[SPEAKER_00]: molecules and atoms and particles and whatever quacks or superstring or quantum vacuum anyway at the end certainly not a table so likewise it is the case of all phenomena and then we come at a point where we can see that is everything is just but labels attached to certain things that comes momentarily together but do not constitute separate entities

08:50.972 --> 08:53.173
[SPEAKER_00]: So therefore, there's no object of grasping either.

08:55.134 --> 09:01.897
[SPEAKER_00]: We cling to a beautiful sort of, I don't know, Ming Dynasty Chinese table, but not to a heap of sawdust.

09:02.978 --> 09:05.679
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless we're a sawdust dealer, then it becomes expensive.

09:06.279 --> 09:07.140
[SPEAKER_00]: Valuable for us.

09:08.901 --> 09:10.742
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, what is a sawdust dealer?

09:11.202 --> 09:17.745
[SPEAKER_00]: There is something for a sawdust dealer, just an annoying heap of sawdust for someone who finds that in his flat.

09:20.107 --> 09:22.248
[SPEAKER_00]: Or like a roses is very...

09:23.649 --> 09:25.190
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a wonderful thing for a poet.

09:25.270 --> 09:28.151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a good salad for a snail.

09:28.272 --> 09:29.972
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's nothing much for a tiger.

09:31.753 --> 09:32.454
[SPEAKER_00]: So likewise.

09:32.854 --> 09:40.318
[SPEAKER_00]: Now if you look at the mind, as Rinpoche said, we might think that the mind is something because we do experience it all the time.

09:41.219 --> 09:43.420
[SPEAKER_00]: But as Rinpoche mentioned, the past thoughts are gone.

09:44.500 --> 09:46.181
[SPEAKER_00]: The future thoughts are not born yet.

09:46.441 --> 09:47.222
[SPEAKER_00]: The present is...

09:48.582 --> 09:50.183
[SPEAKER_00]: never staying in the same place.

09:50.884 --> 09:59.251
[SPEAKER_00]: So where is this mind hanging out between something that does not exist anymore, that does not exist yet and can't grasp it in the present moment.

10:00.792 --> 10:08.619
[SPEAKER_00]: So though there is undeniably some experience, it's not something that we can cast, that we can point out and say, this is the mind.

10:09.780 --> 10:11.581
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a separate entity.

10:12.862 --> 10:14.604
[SPEAKER_00]: So this is analytical meditation.

10:16.807 --> 10:20.690
[SPEAKER_00]: And which leads us to the ultimate nature of thing is what?

10:20.790 --> 10:21.731
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet it appears.

10:21.931 --> 10:23.032
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, it appears.

10:24.233 --> 10:25.614
[SPEAKER_00]: But how does it appear?

10:26.354 --> 10:29.136
[SPEAKER_00]: As something that is devoid of intrinsic existence.

10:29.797 --> 10:34.160
[SPEAKER_00]: So we do not fall neither into nihilism, things that don't exist.

10:34.180 --> 10:35.501
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course they do appear.

10:36.422 --> 10:37.503
[SPEAKER_00]: But how do they appear?

10:38.063 --> 10:41.586
[SPEAKER_00]: Not as something solid, realistic, materialistic.

10:43.087 --> 10:44.428
[SPEAKER_00]: It appears that it is void.

10:45.168 --> 10:56.431
[SPEAKER_00]: So that union of appearance and emptiness is said to be the ultimate view according to the Madhyamika and especially in the Nyingma philosophy, view of the Madhyamika.

10:57.311 --> 11:13.016
[SPEAKER_00]: So then we go through this analytical process until we achieve some kind of clarity and certainty like Chancellor Moshe used to say if we think that there's a thief somewhere in the house and we don't feel easy about that

11:14.301 --> 11:25.383
[SPEAKER_00]: and then maybe I'm just daydreaming, so we look everywhere, open every cupboard, look under every bed, and then when we don't find any teeth, then our mind is at rest.

11:26.363 --> 11:40.806
[SPEAKER_00]: So when we go pursue that in the end, and then we find nothing, and that's when times come for the second aspect that Rinpoche mentioned, that is the contemplative aspect, we gain a kind of clarity,

11:41.813 --> 12:04.052
[SPEAKER_00]: almost like a certainty or at least for the time being we got some certainty compared to before now we need to let that soak in in a way and that the best way is not to pursue the analysis but just to rest in that understanding just like letting it soak in

12:04.740 --> 12:32.033
[SPEAKER_00]: by leaving time, giving it time without further sort of intellectual elaborations so we can try to do that a little bit either taking the example of outer phenomena or taking example of the mind or taking example of the individual self that we cannot find either and if you want to do

12:32.742 --> 12:35.603
[SPEAKER_00]: give the analytical way about the self.

12:36.884 --> 12:39.265
[SPEAKER_00]: We think it's me somewhere, but where?

12:39.826 --> 12:43.227
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly not somewhere in the body, my knees or my heart.

12:44.008 --> 12:45.628
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it diffused throughout the body?

12:45.708 --> 12:46.089
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

12:46.829 --> 12:52.572
[SPEAKER_00]: We can't say that because if we lose our legs, it's still me without legs, but it's not half of me.

12:53.492 --> 12:56.474
[SPEAKER_00]: Just my body is diminished, but my me is still there.

12:57.454 --> 12:58.455
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not outside me.

12:59.980 --> 13:01.601
[SPEAKER_00]: so if it's not in the body then where?

13:01.722 --> 13:26.583
[SPEAKER_00]: maybe in the consciousness and then same reasoning like for the mind is the me in the past in the future that is not born now that we cannot catch so basically we don't find it and then also this notion of me is very often illogical sometimes we say if I push your body you say oh you push me so then you think you identify me with the body and at that moment

13:27.293 --> 13:56.343
[SPEAKER_00]: I say something unpleasant to you, you say you hurt my feelings so now me is my feeling, you hurt me so sometimes it's the mind, sometimes it's the body so that shows that it's neither in any of those so then we can use that analytical either for phenomena or for the mind or for the notion of self and then when we come to a point where we don't find any entity we just rest into that and contemplate this nature

13:57.111 --> 14:10.185
[SPEAKER_00]: of things, of phenomena, of the mind, of the self, which appears yet is devoid of altruistic existence, and try to just rest into that understanding for a few moments.

15:28.790 --> 15:39.396
[SPEAKER_00]: If we think of that, that point of resting is also the same point that is behind the screen of thoughts.

15:41.157 --> 15:54.024
[SPEAKER_00]: When past thoughts have ceased for a moment, we are not yet invaded by future thoughts, and there's a kind of freshness in that present moment, a kind of clear awareness.

15:55.525 --> 15:56.866
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the same quality

15:58.676 --> 15:59.807
[SPEAKER_00]: in which we can rest.

16:59.726 --> 17:05.128
[SPEAKER_00]: In some texts it's called the simplicity, the natural simplicity of the mind.

17:07.449 --> 17:28.758
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a bit like when we have been carrying a heavy load and working in the mountains and at some point we put everything down and sit in front of a big landscape and there's a kind of sudden sort of letting go with not much thought, no hopes, no fear and then just resting

17:29.399 --> 17:31.597
[SPEAKER_00]: in this state of natural simplicity.

18:44.067 --> 19:13.773
[SPEAKER_00]: so what do we do when this state of freshness and resting in the present moment is disturbed by thoughts there's no much point even thinking of stopping them doesn't make sense even because how can you stop something that's already there so you can't prevent it from arising a thought that's already arisen

19:15.232 --> 19:17.253
[SPEAKER_00]: So when they have not a reason, it's not a problem.

19:17.293 --> 19:18.594
[SPEAKER_00]: When they have a reason, they are there.

19:18.694 --> 19:22.217
[SPEAKER_00]: So stopping the thoughts makes no sense.

19:23.578 --> 19:26.019
[SPEAKER_00]: But what do you do when they have a reason?

19:27.840 --> 19:33.964
[SPEAKER_00]: So most of the time, in ordinary times, we get carried out by our thoughts.

19:34.345 --> 19:36.046
[SPEAKER_00]: And they multiply.

19:37.207 --> 19:40.169
[SPEAKER_00]: And they give a kind of chain reaction and invade our mind.

19:40.769 --> 19:42.170
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how we are constantly changing.

19:44.010 --> 20:10.082
[SPEAKER_00]: like under the sway of discursive thoughts but you can as well if you are mindful enough see the thought arising and just let it pass like you let something pass in front of you or like a bird that passes through the sky and doesn't leave any trace so then it's fine

20:11.601 --> 20:14.444
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could that once, and twice, and many times.

20:16.646 --> 20:17.747
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not really difficult.

20:20.149 --> 20:23.973
[SPEAKER_00]: Because all we have to do is to let it come, let it go, and let it vanish.

20:26.055 --> 20:28.116
[SPEAKER_00]: So we can say it's hardship.

20:29.958 --> 20:31.600
[SPEAKER_00]: So let's try to do that for a while.

21:45.426 --> 22:11.396
[SPEAKER_00]: and then if we get used to that then we never lose the recognition that the sky is always there I mentioned like a bird passing through the sky but instead of being sort of obsessed or focused by the bird and seeing nothing but the bird if you see the bird passing through the sky then one bird, two birds, three birds

22:12.062 --> 22:30.540
[SPEAKER_00]: you still see the sky very clearly even birds pass through so therefore the sky is not disturbed it's always you never lose sight of the sky and never distracted by the birds so that's a way to rest in absolute bodhicitta

24:17.962 --> 24:20.463
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, how to connect the two bodhicittas?

24:21.663 --> 24:42.869
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if within that resting in the state of mind, then the perception and the thought of sentient beings comes, and then you realize that because of failing to understand the Absolute Nature, because they grasp and we grasp

24:44.531 --> 24:58.581
[SPEAKER_00]: to solid reality, to the notion of a separate self, to the notion of beautiful and ugly, pleasant and unpleasant, friend and enemy, being endlessly suffer.

25:00.483 --> 25:13.893
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, because of that understanding of Absolute Bodhichitta, then your compassion for the root cause of suffering, not just again the headaches and so forth, or the even dramatic

25:15.750 --> 25:44.541
[SPEAKER_00]: violence and so forth but for the deep cause of ignorance and lack of awareness then very even more powerful compassion will arise for each and every of those sentient beings so the understanding of the Absolute Bodhisattva given altogether greater dimension and depth of two relative Bodhisattva that is to wishing that every being

25:45.109 --> 25:54.031
[SPEAKER_00]: maybe free from suffering, because now you have a better insight about what is the ultimate cause of suffering.

25:54.652 --> 25:59.693
[SPEAKER_00]: So your compassion grows even faster, because it's grounded in wisdom.

26:00.933 --> 26:13.997
[SPEAKER_00]: So in that way, one proceeds from the other, out of absolute bodhicitta, boundless compassion arises, it applies to all sentient beings, and we can train it further through the different methods of mind training.

26:14.785 --> 26:24.237
[SPEAKER_00]: of the relative body citta so let's try to reflect a few moments upon how those two can be thus combined

27:40.464 --> 27:49.186
[SPEAKER_00]: So now, this being the two main aspects, absolute and relative bodhicitta, in the

28:08.252 --> 28:28.658
[SPEAKER_00]: In daily practice there is also many different kinds of meditation methods and visualization which can help day after day to enhance those and bring progress.

28:29.098 --> 28:34.339
[SPEAKER_00]: So there are sort of additional instructions in order to amplify our

28:36.913 --> 28:39.595
[SPEAKER_00]: compassion and bodhichitta and so forth.

28:40.596 --> 28:58.029
[SPEAKER_00]: So one of them which is very much practiced especially in Tibetan Buddhism is called the supreme exchange of giving one's happiness and taking upon ourselves others' suffering.

28:59.009 --> 29:06.235
[SPEAKER_00]: So it happens that some people are a little bit

29:08.710 --> 29:35.470
[SPEAKER_00]: sort of feel strange about this way I even heard I saw once a description of supposed to be a psychological sort of visualization where people say well when you feel a lot of suffering and difficulties and so forth and

29:37.139 --> 30:04.135
[SPEAKER_00]: and tormented within yourself and your physical suffering just imagine that you gather all of them like a sort of like something like tangible thing and suddenly with your breath you expel everything out to the world thank you very much for sending us all that so here is clearly the opposite instead of sending all our trouble outside and tell people just deal with that

30:05.743 --> 30:12.644
[SPEAKER_00]: we do the opposite, but in a way that is not what people might fear, which is to increase our own burden.

30:12.764 --> 30:17.965
[SPEAKER_00]: Another reaction is, how can I ever conceive of taking suffering of others?

30:17.985 --> 30:19.605
[SPEAKER_00]: I have enough on my plate.

30:21.426 --> 30:23.466
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't even deal with my own difficulties.

30:24.146 --> 30:27.607
[SPEAKER_00]: Would you want me again to sacrifice myself and take everything on me?

30:28.047 --> 30:28.887
[SPEAKER_00]: How can that work?

30:29.667 --> 30:34.068
[SPEAKER_00]: But all that comes from a very self-centered perception of

30:34.592 --> 30:38.933
[SPEAKER_00]: suffering of others' suffering and one's own suffering.

30:39.453 --> 30:43.114
[SPEAKER_00]: It's self-centered perception that is usually the cause of your own suffering.

30:44.434 --> 30:54.276
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if you take it from an altruistic perspective, then there's nothing about being discouraged, overburdened, overwhelmed, this feeling that it's not your job and all that.

30:54.616 --> 30:57.456
[SPEAKER_00]: There's unboundless courage.

30:57.476 --> 31:02.537
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that boundless courage is actually a positive quality that

31:03.738 --> 31:28.190
[SPEAKER_00]: lift up actually your strength and your spirit other than discouraging you and also that if you assume other suffering in certain way it's not at all a burden it's a joy and also because you can transform it so how does that practice goes well when we we first visualize all the manifold suffering of sentient beings

31:30.330 --> 31:58.991
[SPEAKER_00]: and then when we think that when we breathe in through the nose usually but whatever it's like we are kind of extracting those suffering out of sentient beings and to give it a more sort of dramatic aspect we think it's like a dark smoke or some kind of dark sort of string that comes to us little bit like when there's a wind sort of movement

31:59.616 --> 32:25.802
[SPEAKER_00]: that the smoke is moves towards you and comes in your nostrils and gathered in your heart but there you visualize your heart as a bright mass of white light and all those sufferings sort of dissolve so they are not burdening you they just transform then when you breathe out having taken and just

32:26.346 --> 32:53.402
[SPEAKER_00]: taken away the suffering from all beings not only the suffering but the causes of suffering all the negative emotions and everything then you send out everything positive that you have all your merit your happiness your capacity or whatever good you did you send that to all beings and each of them receive it fully it's not like you

32:54.041 --> 33:23.541
[SPEAKER_00]: chop a piece of cakes into one million tiny pieces but every being gets the whole thing and then you do this again and again thinking of the different kinds of beings the different kinds of suffering bringing out gathering their suffering it sort of transforms completely into emptiness and then you send out your happiness and do so again and again with great joy

33:24.562 --> 33:36.690
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the practice of giving and taking and so we can do that for a few moments and then I will say a few words about the last variant.

34:28.973 --> 34:57.680
[SPEAKER_00]: So this we can do at any time and also when we do encounter suffering say we are sick or we have a strong pain or we have a strong sadness we can do the same as thinking that oh in the same way that I am sad or that I am in pain there are so many beings who have the same pain even sometimes much harder than mine so may

34:58.942 --> 35:28.217
[SPEAKER_00]: when we do the breathing in may I take away all those sufferings similar to mine add it to mine but strangely enough the fact to add it instead of adding to our burden relieves it because there's a sense of giving it meaning imbuing it with compassion so it actually does seem much lighter to bear and it's not at all increasing our suffering but imbuing it with compassion because

35:28.680 --> 35:30.781
[SPEAKER_00]: We think it stands for others' suffering.

35:32.002 --> 35:33.623
[SPEAKER_00]: That's another way to do so.

35:34.224 --> 35:44.330
[SPEAKER_00]: And the final way, which is also found in the scriptures, is to do the same with mental suffering.

35:44.390 --> 35:58.099
[SPEAKER_00]: That means if people are taken by anger or jealousy, and so to, in the same way, when we're breathing, remove this mental poison from sentient beings, taking upon us

35:58.782 --> 36:11.130
[SPEAKER_00]: transforming it, and sending out the opposite, like loving-kindness if we've taken out anger, rejoicing if we took out jealousy, and so forth.

36:12.450 --> 36:23.257
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's the thing we can do at all times, whatever we do in our activities, when we walk, when we sit somewhere, we can think of that exchange.

36:24.708 --> 36:33.189
[SPEAKER_00]: So now to conclude, before Rinpoche gives blessing to those who want to come and receive his blessing, we do the dedication.

36:33.750 --> 36:51.533
[SPEAKER_00]: And before Rinpoche recites the verses of dedication, the dedication is considered to be like the seal, like when we put at the end of a long discussion, we put a seal of agreement, something that makes it final and solid.

36:52.253 --> 36:54.293
[SPEAKER_00]: So here the reason why we dedicate

36:55.794 --> 37:03.957
[SPEAKER_00]: is to ensure that the benefit of what we have done will continue, will live longer.

37:05.458 --> 37:16.082
[SPEAKER_00]: We say that if we just do a session like today, and we just get up and think of something else, the results are very transient.

37:17.402 --> 37:23.985
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like we say the cactus flower, it flourishes once every many years, and then as soon

37:24.736 --> 37:52.953
[SPEAKER_00]: soon after the cactus has flourished, has bloomed, it dies or it is like a snowflake falling on hot stone, it evaporates right away but if we say, oh may all the merit accrued by this few hours we spent together trying to practice and understand the compassion and loving kindness and the way to engage in that meditation

37:54.444 --> 38:00.308
[SPEAKER_00]: May that in the moment, it alleviates all kinds of present suffering of sentient beings.

38:05.072 --> 38:11.196
[SPEAKER_00]: And in the long term, may it serve so that those beings can attain enlightenment.

38:13.218 --> 38:15.840
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it's like a snowflake falling in a big lake.

38:16.100 --> 38:20.003
[SPEAKER_00]: It will somehow remain as long as the lake remains.

38:20.523 --> 38:23.385
[SPEAKER_00]: So that merit will remain as long as we achieve enlightenment.

38:25.607 --> 38:27.909
[SPEAKER_00]: So may, let's dedicate that.

38:28.169 --> 38:28.409
[SPEAKER_00]: Fr.

38:28.429 --> 38:40.098
[SPEAKER_00]: Moshe will say the verses of dedication and then for a few seconds we will visualize thus dedicating all the merit for the short and the long term benefit of sentient beings.

39:10.208 --> 39:15.070
[SPEAKER_02]: The first question is regarding the meditation of the four immeasurables that was taught earlier on.

39:15.510 --> 39:20.171
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it by repeating each immeasurable by sequence or by visualizing a scene?

39:22.412 --> 39:24.793
[SPEAKER_00]: So the sentences are beneficial, of course.

39:26.733 --> 39:31.615
